<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mireuksa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mireuksa Temple Site</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mireuksa was a Buddhist temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iksan">Iksan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, built under King Mu of Baekje in the early seventh century and among the largest temple complexes of its time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. Its stone pagoda, the oldest and largest surviving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, was restored between 2001 and 2019. A reliquary found inside in 2009 dates its enshrinement to 639. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baekje-historic-areas">Baekje Historic Areas</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Mireuksa was the largest Buddhist temple of the Korean kingdom of Baekje. It was founded in 602 by King Mu, who reigned from 600 to 640. A legend told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-yusa">Samguk yusa</a> says that the king and his queen saw a vision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> at a pond on Yonghwasan, and that the king had the pond drained to build the temple. The nine-storey wooden pagoda at its centre is said to have been the work of the Baekje master craftsman Abiji.</p><p>By 1910 only part of the western stone pagoda still stood, and in 1914 the Japanese government propped it up with concrete. The site was excavated in 1980, revealing much that had been unknown about Baekje architecture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The complex had three pagodas in a line from east to west, a wooden one in the centre flanked by two of stone, each with a hall to its north and enclosed by covered corridors, so that it looked like three temples in the style called one hall, one pagoda. A causeway seems to have led to the outer gate of the walled precinct.</p><p>The western stone pagoda shows how Baekje builders carried the techniques of wooden construction into stone, with a low single-storey base, a massive central pillar and stone supports that imitate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> posts. It now has six storeys, and scholars are unsure how many it once had. In January 2009 a gold plate inscribed in Classical Chinese was found inside it, recording that a queen of King Mu, daughter of Sataek Jeokdeok, had founded the temple and enshrined relics there. Finds of tall foundation stones that once carried wooden floors point to the raised floor later typical of the Korean house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mireuksa is South Korean Historic Site No. 150 and has been partly restored, with a museum on the site. The western pagoda has been a National Treasure since 20 December 1962. Two stone flagpole supports, probably of the Later Silla period, are listed as Treasure No. 236.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mireuksa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…monk Marananta in 384, became the faith of the court, and the Sabi period saw great temples such as Jeongnimsa, whose five-storey stone pagoda still stands, and <b>Mireuksa</b> at Iksan, one of the largest temple complexes of its age, whose reliquary inscription dates its western pagoda to 639.</blockquote><a href="/empire/baekje">Baekje · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
